Date: Thu, 22 Apr 93 12:09:20 +0100
From: Rich Walker <grlw1@cus.cam.ac.uk>
Subject: STEGA: easy veiled messages 

I always liked the old code system of using abook known by the transmitter
and the receiver, and letting that provide your code. Pondering the
transmission of encrypted text invisibly, I came up with a couple of simple
methods based on primitive automatic-text-generating systems, that can be
kocjed out in an hour or two if anyone really needs them.

All the methods rely on using the encrypted message instead of a random
number generator for text generation. Several obvious means of doing so,
which I'm not going to go into.  Then, take the book in use, and scan it to
build up a frequency table. Things that can be used for frequencies:
1. two, three or four letter tuples 
2. two, three ... word tuples 
3. grammatical constructs (given some really interesting software, and an
on-line dictionary) and frequency of words within them.

Then apply an obvious method to generate the text from these.  e.g. 2. for
3-tuples, given two words, there are n possible words following.  Take
rnd(n) to choose: this operation loses no information, and so is reversible
(Note: 'rnd' is, in fact, an appropriate bit of the key, which is itself
the original encrypted message. Got that?)

This, of course, means that you and I can use any book we oth possess to
communicate. Or any large block available on-line. Steganography using the
Gnu C compiler source, tar'd up and gzipped would be mildly amusing; or,
for that matter, using anything out of Project Gutenberg.

Cracking it? Well, I assume the cracker would need to find the book being
used to encrypt the text? And have a copy of it. Or, at least, they'd need
to have the tables extracted from the book. Also, they'd need to notice the
text; presumably, encrypting your text to look like a news.admin.policy
flame-war would pretty much eliminate any chance of anyone ever finding it.
You could even post it there to really confuse the issue...

Rich!

