Depends on the constants of proportionality.  If they dictate an upper
speed, say, in the teraterahertz region, we've a way to go yet.

If there is a fundamental limit on the speed or energy requirements of
reversible computation, it might come from answering the following
question: is there, independently of the nature of the system, a lower
bound on the energy that must be supplied in order to cause a reversible
change of state of a given "magnitude" (however that is measured) to happen
in a given finite time, and to observe that change?  (And can the
observation of the change be reversible?  I suspect there are deep
questions of quantum mechanics here, not just thermodynamics.)

