From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: TECH: Report - Entropic Cost of Computation

Thanks once again to Mike Price for his comments.

> I'm not sure what you mean by "deriving" here.  The formula for S is a
> definition, so we have complete freedom to choose anything we want. 

What I meant was that we want the formal definition of entropy to
have certain properties:  we want it to measure the "uncertainty"
of which microstate a macrostate is in (we want it to be zero when
we know the microstate, and maximum when all are equally probable),
and we want it to be extensional.  The "derivation" I meant was
simply finding such a function --

-C * sum[i] pi ln pi

fits the bill.  A result from information theory says that a
function of this form is the ONLY one that fits such a bill.
[See, e.g., Khinchin's _Mathematical Foundations of Information
Theory_, theorem 1, page 9].

My unhappiness does not involve this, but rather involves the
subsequent step of expressing "uncertainty of microstate" or
(accepting equilibrium arguments) "number of microstates" in
units of energy/temperature.  Also, note that I'm not trying
to be difficult or unreasonably disbelieving of introductory
thermodynamics; I become more convinced by the day.... I'm
just trying to understand so that I can replace the lamentation
in my previous essay with a concrete explanation of quantitative
entropic costs of phase space volume reduction.

Your alternatives also seem to do the same thing -- slide that "k"
in (with units) at some point "purely for our convenience".  Don't
reply to this point for now, if you don't want, though; I'm planning to
go see a thermo prof here at UW about it; I feel that a shorter
round-trip time will be helpful in clearing up my problem.  I
also want to ask why we couldn't use a base-2 log in the definition
of S, which would seem (probably doesn't but I don't know why) to
change the cost of bit erasure.

> At equilibrium S is maximised

On this point, the text I was using defined the approach to equilibrium
is the approach to SOME constant value of S; not necessarily the
maximum, which was the source of my question.  I got another rather
different book that presented it this way (paraphrased):

Postulate: at equilibrium, all the p[i] are equal.  This just seems
to be true.  Nobody doubts it.  It works.  Most of the rest of the
book is based on it, so if you don't believe it, throw the book away.
But you should believe it.  It is equivalent to the second law of
thermodynamics.

So OK, my skepticism has limits, and I'll accept it.

> A reversible nanocomputer (or any reversible mechanism) *is* in
> equilibrium.

Hmm.  I thought you defined equilibrium as "all microstates (exact
particle positions) equally probable."  But while it's computing,
the machine state changes... I suppose that we could ignore the
state transitions and just deal with individual states... I agree
that while in a particular state the machine is in equilibrium
(if the microstate probabilities are equal in the start state, they
have to be for all subsequent states).  Though it makes me a bit
uncomfortable, I guess I can live with it.

derek
