Date: Thu, 22 Apr 1993 02:57:14 -0700 (PDT)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: EPIST: Why do formal systems work?

Dan Davis:
> Whatever system provides the best predictive power is the one to use,
> regardless of the number of entities involved. 

This disregards computational speed, which is critically
important, in terms of human learning curves as well as
machine computation.  Scientists must trade off predictive
power vs. the ability to understanding, communicate and use
theory.  The rule of thumb Occam's Razor catches this nicely.  

For example, epicycles had quite good predictive power, but they 
were too cumbersome to work with, and were replaced by the simpler
Copernican model and Kepler's laws.  This in turn reduced 
computational complexity to the point where Newton could build
on the model to develop laws of gravitation, etc.  Newton's
laws of gravitation might have been derived from epicycle theory,
but it would have been *much* more complex, so much so that
the ideas probably would not have occured to Newton, perhaps
would not have emerged until the era of modern computers, which would
likely be needed to crunch the differential equations falling out of 
the epicycles.

Logarithms are another example of reducing computational complexity.  
I might go so far as to say that the goal of science is to 
minimize the computational complexity of predicting the world.

> By what standard is one thing simpler than another? 

Two standards might be used: entropy (in information theory)
and computational complexity.  Neither is arbitary.

In fact, I would be *awfully* surprised if somebody hasn't
formalized Occam's Razor in terms of information theory
or computational complexity.  Anybody know of such work?

